  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The corrugated bolete (Hemileccinum hortonii) is a medium-sized mushroom belonging to the bolete family, characterized by its distinctive ridged or corrugated pores beneath the cap. Found in New England forests, it is a choice edible mushroom when properly prepared.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Corrugated Pores",
      "description": "The pores beneath the cap are distinctly ridged, angular, and somewhat maze-like rather than round and smooth like many boletes",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Appearance",
      "description": "Brown to tan colored cap with a dry, matte surface; cap typically 2-4 inches in diameter",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Characteristics",
      "description": "Pale stem with fine reddish or brownish reticulation (net-like pattern) in the upper portion",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This bolete is found in mixed deciduous and coniferous forests throughout New England, often appearing in summer and fall near oak, birch, and conifer trees.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The corrugated bolete's unique maze-like pore structure sets it apart from most other boletes and makes it one of the more visually distinctive species in the region.",
